#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148231} :activity/id #uuid "8613c196-881e-55db-8570-a966119ce38b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050518 :topic/id #uuid "501b069b-b384-57af-b245-3461172e72ed" :topic/math-academy-id 2475} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148231 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148794 :tutorial/content "Exponents often show up in expressions with other operations. To evaluate these expressions, we follow the **order of operations**.\n\nThe order of the operations we perform follows the acronym PEMDAS:\n\n- First, we calculate any operations inside **P**arentheses, $().$\n\n- Next, we evaluate the **E**xponents $(a^b).$\n\n- Then, we perform the **M**ultiplications ($\\times$) and **D**ivisions ($\\div$).\n\n- Finally, we complete any **A**dditions ($+$) and **S**ubtractions ($-$).\n\nFor example, let's look at the following expression:\n\n$$\n3 \\times 2^2\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. Here, we do not have **P**arentheses, so we move on to **E**xponents:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\times\\overbrace{2^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 4}  = 3\\times \\color{blue} 4\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we perform the **M**ultiplication:\n\n$$\n3\\times 4 = 12\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n3 \\times 2^2=12.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dc2c876f-37fd-5018-8051-7eba438302c9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8808 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b90d723e-3bc0-5ce9-a29a-6ce075f06b65" :step/math-academy-id 131988 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148232}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148232 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147394 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147173 :question/id #uuid "6d89587f-dacf-5ce3-95f0-9dd51d307af8" :question/math-academy-id "e-14188"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b754e55b-59fa-50de-b2f9-0444c3177041" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049368}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592